# Cloudflare Pages security headers (Cloudflare ignores vercel.json).
# The Content-Security-Policy below was validated against the built app:
# script-src 'self' — the app itself ships no inline/eval scripts. The single
# sha256 allow-list is Furo's theme-init inline <script> on the wiki (/wiki) —
# `document.body.dataset.theme = ...`, needed so the dark/light toggle works
# under this CSP; it's a fixed, theme-pinned snippet (see wiki/requirements.txt —
# recompute if the theme bumps).
# Other allowances are exactly what the app needs — WebSocket transport (ws:/wss:),
# three.js GLTF buffers fetched as data: URIs, the OpenStreetMap GPS-map iframe,
# OpenStreetMap raster tiles for the no-GPS calibration map picker (img-src), and
# inline <style> for dynamic widget sizing.
# connect-src also allows https: and http://localhost for the AI Assistant's
# bring-your-own-key model calls (Anthropic/OpenAI/OpenAI-compatible gateways over
# https, a local Ollama over http://localhost). Consistent with the already-open
# ws:/wss:. This app sends no analytics or usage data anywhere, so the only
# outbound connections are ever to the user's own configured endpoints.
# Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy and -Embedder-Policy make this page
# cross-origin isolated, which is what gives it SharedArrayBuffer. The
# WebAssembly simulator needs that: ArduPilot's build runs its main loop on a
# worker (-sPROXY_TO_PTHREAD), because that loop blocks and would otherwise
# freeze the tab.
#
# Isolation cannot be scoped to one tab -- it is a property of the whole
# document, and an isolated iframe inside a non-isolated page gets nothing --
# so it applies site-wide, and the two cross-origin things this app used had
# to stop being cross-origin first:
#   - map tiles now come from /tiles on this origin (functions/tiles)
#   - the live GPS map draws with Leaflet instead of embedding openstreetmap.org
# Both allowances are therefore gone from the CSP below. worker-src gains blob:
# because Emscripten starts its pthread workers from a blob URL.
#
# The service worker must never be cached, so browsers pick up the
# self-destructing sw.js on the next visit and un-stick users promptly.
/sw.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache, no-store, must-revalidate

/*
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: no-referrer
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
  Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy: require-corp
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'wasm-unsafe-eval' 'sha256-ySvT2PEZeueHGC1y2crNuNTfphBynFPP7i+U21fEgX0='; connect-src 'self' ws: wss: data: https: http://localhost:* http://127.0.0.1:*; img-src 'self' data: blob:; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; font-src 'self' data:; worker-src 'self' blob:; manifest-src 'self'; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'

# No per-path rule for /sitl/. Cloudflare APPENDS the headers of every matching
# rule rather than replacing them, so a /sitl/* block on top of /* produced two
# Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy headers on the same response. fetch() is lenient
# about that and reported require-corp, but the worker-script check parses COEP
# strictly: a doubled value is not a valid one, so it counted as absent and the
# pthread worker was blocked -- the "starting worker threads" hang.
